  "audit_question": {
    "disposition": null,
    "preferred_check": "source_review",
    "requests": [
      {
        "file": "testing.go",
        "start_line": 1,
        "end_line": 300,
        "reason": "Inspect repository FSM and snapshot helpers for concrete Persist implementations and their handling of Write, Close and Cancel results. Determine whether repository usage supplies additional evidence about intended terminal-error ownership. Such helpers are implementation examples, not independently normative contracts or proof of deployment applicability."
      }
    ],
    "question": "For the principal fact F_snapshot_close_accepted, does takeSnapshot's consumption of successful Persist and subsequent Close returns justify recording snapshot metadata and requesting log compaction when an earlier terminal call on the same FileSnapshotSink failed or cancelled publication, or does the applicable Persist/sink contract exclude that history?",
    "importance": "Acceptance without usable replacement snapshot material could permit removal of history needed for restart or replica recovery. The immediate discriminator is whether the unsuccessful terminal history is permitted at the Persist boundary; neither legal reachability nor actual recovery loss has been established.",
    "source_ids": [
      "fsm.go:1:80",
      "snapshot.go:1:278",
      "snapshot.go:125:278",
      "file_snapshot.go:397:500"
    ],
    "participants": [
      "takeSnapshot caller",
      "Application FSMSnapshot implementation",
      "FileSnapshotSink"
    ],
    "objects": [
      "Snapshot sink object",
      "Published snapshot",
      "Snapshot progress metadata",
      "Log interval eligible for compaction"
    ],
    "contexts": [
      "Built-in file snapshot store when selected",
      "Sequential terminal calls on the same sink",
      "Snapshot persistence and publication errors"
    ],
    "event_paths": [
      "takeSnapshot -> FSMSnapshot.Persist -> sink terminal call -> takeSnapshot Close -> snapshot metadata update and compaction"
    ],
    "activity_classes": [
      "A6"
    ],
    "behavior_ids": [
      "B_snapshot_orchestration",
      "B_file_snapshot_close",
      "file_snapshot_close_terminal_path",
      "file_snapshot_cancel_first",
      "file_snapshot_cancel_already_closed",
      "B_log_compaction"
    ],
    "fact_ids": [
      "F_snapshot_close_accepted"
    ],
    "obligation_relation_kind": "consumption",
    "counterevidence": [
      "The acquired FSMSnapshot.Persist contract assigns writing necessary state and calling Close on completion, or Cancel on error, to the FSM implementation. SnapshotSink repeats this completion/error distinction. Thus cancellation followed by successful Persist cannot simply be assumed to be a permitted history.",
      "takeSnapshot checks Persist's returned error and exits before metadata update or compaction when it is non-nil. It separately checks its subsequent Close result.",
      "The first FileSnapshotSink.Close success path performs finalization, metadata writing, rename, applicable parent synchronization and retention before returning nil.",
      "The closed guard explicitly implements idempotent terminal calls. A repeated nil result alone does not establish a contract requiring renewed publication or preservation of the first error.",
      "Compaction bounds deletion by the snapshot index and trailing-log configuration and may skip deletion. Acceptance and compaction invocation alone do not demonstrate loss of required recovery history."
    ],
    "unknowns": [
      "The interface comments establish terminal-call ownership but do not explicitly specify whether Persist must propagate a Close error, whether terminal errors are sticky, or what a repeated Close return guarantees.",
      "Whether a permitted Persist implementation can return nil after an unsuccessful or cancelling terminal call remains unresolved.",
      "Whether repository Persist implementations clarify intended handling of terminal errors without depending on unspecified application behavior.",
      "Whether an independently applicable local contract requires FileSnapshotSink to retain terminal errors or reject Close after cancellation; the observed latch behavior alone does not establish that requirement.",
      "Whether a legally reachable acceptance without usable publication causes deletion of history required for recovery under retained snapshots and the trailing-log configuration.",
      "The actual selected application snapshot implementation, snapshot store and filesystem configuration."
